  "response": "I have completed my performance review of the Scheme files. Below are the identified issues, focusing on algorithmic complexity, unnecessary allocations, and inefficient data structures.\n\n```json\n[\n  {\n    \"file\": \"shell/ui/layout.ss\",\n    \"severity\": \"high\",\n    \"line\": 75,\n    \"description\": \"The pure 'canvas-set' function performs a 'vector-copy' of the entire canvas cells for every single character change. Drawing operations like 'draw-string' or 'fill-rect' that call this in a loop will have O(N*M) complexity where N is canvas size and M is pixels changed, leading to massive unnecessary allocations.\",\n    \"code_snippet\": \"(define (canvas-set c x y ch)\\n  ...\\n           (let ([cells (vector-copy (canvas-cells c))]\\n                 [idx (+ (* y w) x)])\\n                (vector-set! cells idx ch)\\n                (make-canvas% w h cells)))))\",\n    \"suggested_fix\": \"Use a persistent data structure like a 2D segment tree or a sparse matrix representation, or provide a batch-update function that copies the vector once for multiple changes. Alternatively, encourage the use of 'canvas-set!' for hot paths.\",\n    \"confidence\": 0.95,\n    \"category\": \"performance\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"shell/lens/test-finder.ss\",\n    \"severity\": \"high\",\n    \"line\": 142,\n    \"description\": \"'search-all-tests-for' performs a linear scan over every test file in the codebase, reading and parsing each one to find symbol references. This involves significant I/O and CPU overhead that scales linearly with the number of test files.\",\n    \"code_snippet\": \"(define (search-all-tests-for sym)\\n  (let ([test-files (find-all-test-files)])\\n       (filter-map\\n        (lambda (test-path)\\n                (and (test-file-references-symbol? test-path sym)\\n                     (list test-path 1 (extract-test-names test-path))))\\n        test-files)))\",\n    \"suggested_fix\": \"Implement a cross-reference index for